{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310830,"nonce":"9D+nSjn66a+1N1kseYyPkh18WnI=","title":"Phased plan for a `rayzor debug` tool that maps JIT crashes back to Haxe source","message":"**Why:** Rayzor JITs Haxe to native via Cranelift (and to WASM). When\nJIT'd code crashes, all the user gets is a SIGILL / SIGSEGV exit code\nand silence — sometimes not even a runtime trace line, because the\ncrash beats `main()` to the first trace. Debugging requires bespoke\nlldb scripting per incident (see this session's investigation of\n`bugs_gguf_tensor_decode_crash`: ~6 lldb invocations to identify a\nsingle trap address). A first-class `rayzor debug` subcommand pays\nfor itself the first time it's used.\n\n\n- **Shadow-stack frame metadata** — `rayzor_update_call_frame_location`\n  is emitted at every call site with the calling line/col. Used today\n  by exception `details()` for the \"Called from\" frames (see\n  `bugs_stack_traces_disabled`).\n- **Function-pointer table** —\n  `TieredBackend::function_pointers: BTreeMap\u003cIrFunctionId, usize\u003e`\n  maps each function to its JIT'd entry address. Lets us go\n  `address → IrFunctionId → qualified name`.\n- **Source-info registration** —\n  `TieredBackend::register_source_info_for_pointers` already\n  associates JIT addresses with the originating IR functions for\n  llvm-promotion. Same mechanism extends to address→source mapping.\n- **The IR `Trap` instruction** — when we emit our own traps\n  (overflow checks, indirect-call signature mismatch, unreachable)\n  we can choose the trap-code immediate and look it up on crash.\n\n\n- Spawn `rayzor run \u003cfile\u003e` under `lldb -batch`, configure SIGILL /\n  SIGSEGV / SIGBUS to break.\n- On break: capture PC, LR, x0..x7 (or rdi..r9 on x86), full\n  backtrace.\n- Symbolicate the PC against `function_pointers`: which IR function\n  did we crash inside? At what byte offset from its entry?\n- Print: trap kind, IR func qualified name, source file:line for the\n  call site (from `rayzor::run_file + N` frame), the JIT entry's\n  alignment relative to its own page (caught the off-by-4 leading\n  `udf` problem this session).\n- ~200-300 LOC + a short Python lldb script. Ship under\n  `compiler/src/bin/rayzor_debug.rs`.\n\n- Walk the shadow-stack frames at the time of crash (read the same\n  per-thread frame array the exception path uses).\n- Print user-readable \"at Main.main (Main.hx:23:5) / at\n  GGUFLoader.tensorsFromReader (GGUFLoader.hx:147:9) / …\" instead of\n  raw Cranelift offsets.\n- Requires extern access to the shadow-stack pointer from the\n  debugger context (lldb python can `frame.EvaluateExpression` to\n  read globals).\n\n- Centralise the trap-code → meaning table in\n  `compiler/src/codegen/trap_codes.rs` (currently scattered: some\n  cranelift built-ins, some rayzor-emitted).\n- `rayzor debug` looks up the immediate of the `udf` (ARM64) /\n  `ud2` (x86) and prints the human-readable cause:\n  \"indirect call to null fn-ptr\", \"i32-to-int conversion out of\n  range\", \"explicit Haxe `throw` without handler\", etc.\n- Distinguish \"Cranelift safety trap\" from \"user trap\" from\n  \"function-pointer alignment / tombstone\" — the latter (this\n  session's case) gets a distinctive hint.\n\n- Drop into an interactive REPL on crash. Commands:\n  - `bt` — Haxe backtrace\n  - `frame N` — switch frame\n  - `print \u003cname\u003e` — evaluate a variable by walking the local\n    table (would need DWARF-like info we don't emit yet, or read\n    via shadow-stack locals).\n  - `disasm` — show disassembly around PC with Haxe source\n    interleaved.\n- This is the \"step debugger\" version. Big surface area; only\n  build after Phases 1-3 prove the symbol/source mapping works.\n\n- Set source breakpoints. Map source line → JIT address(es) via the\n  shadow-stack metadata (currently call-site only; would need\n  per-statement source ranges, which TAST already tracks).\n- Step over / step in.\n- Largest scope; not needed for the crash-investigation use case\n  this roadmap was born from.\n\n- DWARF / source-map emission for the WASM target.\n- `rayzor debug --target wasm` opens a Chromium-compatible\n  source-mapped session.\n- Pre-req: wasm_backend doesn't emit source-maps today. Treat as\n  a follow-on of the WASM roadmap, not this one.\n\n\nThis session burned a lot of time on ad-hoc lldb to discover **one\nfact**: the llama-chat JIT calls a function whose first instruction\nis `udf #0xc11f` (4 bytes before the real prologue at `pacibsp`).\nPhase 1 would have surfaced that in a single command, with the\ntrap immediate decoded, the responsible IR function named, and the\ncaller's source line printed. From there, the JIT-install bug is a\nmuch shorter investigation.\n\n\n- **JIT-page lookup may not be 1:1 with function entries.** Cranelift\n  emits multiple functions per page; need to consult the symbol map\n  not just round the PC down. Solvable but worth noting.\n- **lldb dep on macOS comes with Xcode CLT.** Acceptable for dev\n  builds; for distributed binaries we'd ship our own ptrace shim.\n- **Shadow stack is per-thread.** Async / Future / worker threads\n  need their own shadow stacks already (they do); the debugger needs\n  to know which thread to read.\n\nRelated: [[bugs_gguf_tensor_decode_crash]] (the bug that motivated\nthis roadmap), [[bugs_stack_traces_disabled]] (shadow-stack metadata\ninfrastructure we'll reuse).\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `project_debugger_roadmap` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}